 {"id":"CF-158","title":"P3 \u2014 the CF-119 lock adjudicator reports HELD for any process that merely OPENS the path: an open fd and a SHARED flock are not ownership, so the phantom stand-down returns through the new instrument","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T03:52:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T03:52:00Z","detail":{"filed_by":"review lane","tick":"code-factory-review-2026-11-04-03-41-13","reviewed_commit":"a012d02180c1ea53e979b65b545d9533f7d2da07","reviewed_tip":"d3b06a8fed3010f7e1eaea84f12baba1d6c6b1ea","first_reviewed_tip":"7989ba3a7eb45a1adafc2bc5d5b4c0831e412d38","gate_at_filing":"bash tests/selftest.sh -> SELFTEST: PASS, rc=0, 103 PASS rows, at d3b06a8 (re-run at the filing tip; the same tree passed at 7989ba3 before a sibling landed a CF-156 closeout, and git diff --name-only 7989ba3..d3b06a8 -- scripts/ tests/ .gitignore prints 0 files)","transport":"throwaway clone of origin/main at 7989ba3 in tick scratch, fast-forwarded to d3b06a8 and re-probed before filing; registered workdir /home/kara/code-factory untouched (attached main, parity 0/0, zero tracked edits, no operation markers)","evidence_artifact":"docs/audits/code-factory-review-CF-158.md"},"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04T03:4x Z by review tick code-factory-review-2026-11-04-03-41-13 on a throwaway clone of origin/main at 7989ba3a7eb45a1adafc2bc5d5b4c0831e412d38. The reviewed commit is a012d02180c1ea53e979b65b545d9533f7d2da07 ('fix(board-lock): adjudicate board/.lock as residue and ship the holder-proof classifier', 413 insertions / 0 deletions, closes CF-119, tier1 PASS, tier2 PASS run c32a220c).\n\nIts load-bearing claim is 'HELD only on a proven live holder (a /proc fd scan excluding self, or a non-blocking exclusive flock probe failing EWOULDBLOCK); RESIDUE otherwise'. Both proofs over-accept. (a) An OPEN FD is not a lock: with a process that merely opens the path \u2014 ( exec 3< \"$S/.lock\"; sleep ) & and no flock at all \u2014 the shipped classifier prints 'HELD holder=pid-2711809'; the same arm inside the clone (so the count resolves) prints 'HELD holder=pid-2712037' while its unheld control prints 'RESIDUE consumers=0 commits_after_mtime=0'. Any reader (tail -f, an editor, another tool) therefore tells every lane to WAIT, which is the phantom ownership barrier CF-119 was filed to remove, re-entered through the instrument built to remove it. (b) A SHARED lock is not ownership either: a holder taking fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_SH) prints 'HELD holder=pid-2747535', and two shared holders demonstrably coexist (r1=yes r2=yes), so the exclusive probe's EWOULDBLOCK also fires on a lock that excludes nobody. HELD thus conflates an opener, a shared reader and an exclusive holder.\n\nThe gate cannot see either: replacing the fd branch with a constant (sed 's/^    holders = fd_holders(lock)$/    holders = []  # MUTATION/') leaves SELFTEST: PASS rc=0 and both section-33 rows green ('HELD holder=flock-eagain'), because the section's HELD arm holds a real flock, which satisfies either proof. A fixture that asserts only the behaviour both implementations share is mutation-blind on the branch that manufactures the false positive.\n\nThe consumer count is a scope claim, not a repository claim: consumer_count() globs scripts/boardctl* while CF-119's own derivation grepped all of scripts/. A flock user at scripts/__scope_probe.sh still prints 'RESIDUE consumers=0'; the same content at scripts/boardctl_scope_probe.sh prints 'consumers=1' (control). So 'consumers=0' certifies one filename prefix, and a future writer at scripts/write_board.py would be invisible while the verdict reads RESIDUE.\n\nDoc leg: docs/BOARD.md:57-59 describes the code faithfully \u2014 'a live process holds an open fd on the path, or a non-blocking exclusive flock probe failed with EWOULDBLOCK. A held lock means wait.' The page's own premise ('a present lock file is not proof of a held lock') is contradicted by the classifier for any reader, and the doctrine line turns the false positive into a stand-down instruction, so the drift is in the DEFINITION of HELD the doc inherits, not in its description of the implementation.\n\nNot a duplicate and not a reopening: CF-119 is complete and filed the residue adjudication itself; its AC-2 named 'an fd probe on /proc/<pid>/fd' as an acceptable holder proof, so the defect is in that proof's definition. A class census of the board for 'board_lock|board lock|HELD|holder|fd scan' returns no other row, and the two other large implementation commits of the window hold up (23c1c6d refuses an unset CRIER_URL at scripts/node-create.sh:69 before the spawn at line 112; 875bc72 moves the install log to $HOME/.hermes-install-$PORT.XXXXXXXX.log at scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:86).","acceptance_criteria":"1. A process that holds an OPEN FD on the lock path but no flock is not reported HELD. Reproduce on the current tree with the row's probe: it prints 'unheld=RESIDUE consumers=0 commits_after_mtime=unknown' then 'reader_only=HELD holder=pid-<n>' then 'shared_lock=HELD holder=pid-<n>' and exits 0. After the fix the reader arm must print a non-HELD verdict \u2014 RESIDUE, or a distinct state (e.g. 'OPEN holder=pid-<n>') that docs/BOARD.md explicitly defines as proceed \u2014 and the row/commit must quote that verbatim output.\n2. A SHARED flock holder is not reported HELD: fcntl.flock(fd, LOCK_SH), with a second shared holder alive at the same time, must not print HELD; a lock that two readers hold concurrently is not an ownership barrier.\n3. tests/selftest.sh section 33 is no longer mutation-blind: disabling the surviving holder-proof branch must turn the section RED. Today replacing 'holders = fd_holders(lock)' with 'holders = []' leaves SELFTEST: PASS and both section-33 rows green. The row must state that mutation as a re-runnable command and show the red and then the green run.\n4. The consumer count cannot print consumers=0 while a shipped script holds a lock: either every flock|fcntl user under scripts/ is counted, or the verdict names the scan scope it used and docs/BOARD.md states that scope. Today a flock user at scripts/__scope_probe.sh yields consumers=0 while the same content at scripts/boardctl_scope_probe.sh yields consumers=1.\n5. docs/BOARD.md's verdict legend is corrected in the same change: the held / opener / shared distinction is stated, and the sentence 'A held lock means wait' no longer covers a process that merely opens the file. bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS with the new arms visible as named PASS rows and bash scripts/boardctl validate prints RESULT: OK.","probe":"S=$(mktemp -d); : > \"$S/.lock\"; printf 'unheld='; python3 -B scripts/board_lock.py \"$S/.lock\"; ( exec 3< \"$S/.lock\"; sleep 3 ) & R=$!; sleep 0.4; printf 'reader_only='; python3 -B scripts/board_lock.py \"$S/.lock\"; kill $R 2>/dev/null; wait $R 2>/dev/null; ( flock -s 9; sleep 3 ) 9<\"$S/.lock\" & H=$!; sleep 0.5; printf 'shared_lock='; python3 -B scripts/board_lock.py \"$S/.lock\"; kill $H 2>/dev/null; wait $H 2>/dev/null; rm -rf \"$S\""}
